    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Bay windows are among the loveliest features a home can have. They bring in plenty of natural light, offer wide views and create a cosy alcove that can become a reading nook or a breakfast spot. In a rental, though, they often become a source of design frustration. Most bay windows call for specialist hardware that usually means drilling several holes into the walls, ceiling or window frames, which many landlords will not allow.</p>
<p>The challenge lies in the angles. Standard curtain poles do not bend, and the geometry of a bay usually demands a tailored solution. But renting should not mean giving up privacy or living with bare, echoing glass. With the right damage-free fixings and a little creative styling, you can dress a bay window so it looks considered and finished, while protecting your deposit. Before you start, check your tenancy agreement, and if you want to add any fixings, get your landlord’s permission in writing. (If your problem is a window with no room above or beside it, see our separate guide to a <a href="/blog/curtain-rod-for-window-with-no-wall-space">curtain rod for a window with no wall space</a>.)</p>

<h2 id="tension-rods">1. Tension rods</h2>
<p>Tension rods are one of the most useful tools for renters. They work on a spring-loaded or screw-adjusted mechanism that pushes outwards against the sides of the window reveal, staying in place through friction alone. For a typical three-sided bay, you will need three separate tension rods, one for each section of glass.</p>
<p>This method works best if your windows are recessed, meaning there is a reveal or frame deep and solid enough to hold the rod. Placing a rod in each section creates a clean, minimal look that stays within the architectural lines of the window, and you can fit or remove the rods in seconds. Look for sturdier versions with grippy rubber end caps, check the maker’s maximum span (long rods sag more), and test on a hidden spot first, as pressure can mark soft paint. If you are worried about rods slipping under heavier fabric, keep your curtains light: linens, sheers or lightweight cotton.</p>

<h2 id="adhesive-hooks">2. Adhesive hooks</h2>
<p>When you cannot drill, adhesive hooks can help, within limits. Check the weight rating on the packaging, and remember that adhesive hooks suit only light rods and sheer curtains, never heavy lined or blackout fabric. To use this method in a bay window, choose slim, lightweight curtain rods and place the hooks at the points where the window angles meet.</p>
<p>This setup lets the rod sit just above the window frame. Because you are not limited by the internal width of the window, as you are with tension rods, you can fix the hooks on the wall above the window to create an impression of extra height. For a three-sided bay, you might use four hooks: one at each far end and two at the inner corners of the bay, holding three short rods that meet at the corners. Clean the surface first, follow the maker’s instructions for curing time, and test removal on a hidden spot, because some paints lift away with the adhesive.</p>

<h2 id="flexible-tracks">3. Flexible tracks</h2>
<!--figure:double-height-sheer-curtains|Tall sheer curtains falling from ceiling level in a double-height living room, the kind of effect a ceiling-mounted track creates.-->
<p>If you want a seamless look that lets your curtains glide around the corners of the bay without snagging, a flexible curtain track is the answer. These are usually screwed into the ceiling. Adhesive mounting is only an option for very light tracks carrying sheer curtains, and only if the track’s manufacturer approves it; otherwise, assume a track needs screws.</p>
<p>A flexible track is made from a bendable plastic or metal profile that can be shaped to the exact angles of your bay. This removes the gaps you get between separate rods, so when the curtains are closed they form a continuous wall of fabric for better light control and privacy. If your landlord agrees in writing to a few small holes in the ceiling, which are often easier to fill and repaint than holes in a reveal, a handful of ceiling screws gives a permanent-looking result that can be made good when you leave. On an older textured ceiling, check for asbestos before drilling; our guide to <a href="/blog/hide-popcorn-ceiling-renting">popcorn ceilings in a rental</a> explains why.</p>

<h2 id="twist-and-fit-rods">4. Twist-and-fit rods</h2>
<p>Twist-and-fit rods are a development of the standard tension rod. Instead of sitting inside the reveal, they are designed to grip the outer window frame or trim, using tension or a clamp to hold on and providing a stable base for your curtains.</p>
<p>They work well where the window frame is sturdy, but frame compatibility is everything: check the bracket’s stated frame depth and profile against your window before you buy, and make sure the window still opens and closes properly once it is fitted. Some systems offer corner connectors or elbow joints that join rods at set angles (commonly 90 or 135 degrees), creating a single rod that follows the shape of the bay. Because these rods sit outside the reveal, curtains can cover the whole window and reduce light leaking around the sides; just keep heavier lined or blackout curtains within the rod’s stated limits.</p>

<h2 id="sheer-fabrics">5. Sheer fabrics</h2>
<!--figure:fluted-walls-sheer-curtains-lounge|Floor-length sheer curtains soften a lounge with fluted walls; light sheers like these suit tension rods and slim rental-friendly rods.-->
<p>When it comes to choosing fabric for a rental bay window, sheers are often the smartest option. They are very light, which puts minimal stress on tension rods or adhesive hooks and greatly reduces the risk of the whole lot coming down in the night.</p>
<p>Sheers also suit bay windows because they celebrate the light. They give a layer of privacy, stopping neighbours from seeing straight in during the day, while keeping the room bright and airy. Layer several sheer panels for a fuller, more luxurious look. In a bay, gathering the fabric generously on the rods adds texture and hides the hardware, so the whole arrangement looks more intentional.</p>

<h2 id="measuring-angles">6. Measuring angles</h2>
<p>The biggest mistake renters make is guessing the width of a bay window. Because of the angles, the front width of the bay is different from the back width closest to the glass, so measure each section individually.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Each section:</strong> measure the centre window and then the two side windows, along the line where the rods or track will sit.</li>
<li><strong>Tension rods:</strong> measure the inside width of the reveal at the top, middle and bottom, as older windows are rarely perfectly square.</li>
<li><strong>Hooks or a track:</strong> measure the outside width where you want the fabric to hang, and note each angle if you are ordering a bendable track.</li>
<li><strong>Drop:</strong> measure from your fixing point to the floor. For a puddled look, add 5–8 cm (2–3 in); for a hovering look, subtract about 1 cm (½ in) so the curtains clear the floor and do not collect dust.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Use a metal tape measure rather than a fabric one, and write down every measurement next to a quick sketch of the bay. Bays and older frames are rarely as symmetrical as they look.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="strategic-layering">7. Strategic layering</h2>
<p>If you need the light-blocking power of heavy curtains but are worried about the weight on rental-friendly hardware, consider a layered approach. Use tension rods for the inner sheers, which can stay closed most of the time for privacy, and hang heavier decorative panels only at the two outer ends of the bay. Those heavier panels need a fixing that can genuinely take their weight, such as a sturdy twist-and-fit rod or, with permission, a properly fixed pole; freestanding curtain stands are another drill-free option where floor space allows.</p>
<p>Placing the heavier, darker panels only on the outermost edges frames the bay without loading a single rod, gives the window a sense of grandeur and helps with insulation in winter. Magnetic tie-backs can hold the heavy panels open and add a touch of elegance without screwing hardware into the wall.</p>

<h2 id="cafe-curtains">8. Café curtains</h2>
<p>For many renters the goal is not total darkness but privacy from the neighbourhood. If you live on a ground floor or a busy street, café curtains are a clever solution for bay windows. Placing tension rods halfway up the window blocks the line of sight from the street while leaving the top half open to sunlight and views of the sky.</p>
<p>This is a very light setup, which makes it one of the safest options for tension rods. Café curtains also use less fabric, an economical way to dress a large window. In a bay, they create a charming, bistro-style feel that works especially well in kitchens and dining nooks.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>A bay window in a rental does not have to stay bare. Tension rods in each section, light sheers, careful measuring and a little layering will cover most bays without a single hole. Where you want the neatest finish of a flexible track or a properly fixed pole, ask your landlord, get the answer in writing and keep heavier fabrics on fixings rated to carry them. Whatever you choose, test adhesives and pressure points on a hidden spot first, and your deposit stays safe.</p>

<h2 id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3 id="single-long-rod">Can I use a single long curtain rod for a bay window?</h3>
<p>A standard straight rod will not work because it cannot follow the angles of the window. You need either separate rods for each section or a bay window rod with angled elbow joints. For renters, separate tension rods are usually the easiest and most damage-free option.</p>
<h3 id="light-leaking-corners">How do I stop light leaking through the gaps in the corners?</h3>
<p>Gaps appear where separate rods meet. To minimise them, overlap your curtain panels at each corner, or push the fabric as close to the corner as possible. A flexible track is the only way to remove these gaps completely.</p>
<h3 id="tension-rods-paint">Will tension rods damage the paint in my rental?</h3>
<p>Usually not, as tension rods have rubber feet designed to protect surfaces. However, if they are overtightened or the paint is soft or already flaking, they can leave a mark. Test on a hidden spot first, avoid overtightening, and consider a small felt pad between the rod end and the reveal.</p>
<h3 id="best-curtain-length">What is the best curtain length for a bay window?</h3>
<p>It depends on your furniture. If you have a window seat or a desk in the bay, sill-length curtains that finish at the window ledge are most practical. If the area is clear, floor-length curtains look more polished and help the room feel taller. Keep curtains clear of any radiator below the window.</p>
<h3 id="adhesive-hooks-weight">How much weight can adhesive hooks really hold?</h3>
<p>It varies widely by product, so always check the rating on the packaging and follow it. Adhesive hooks suit only light rods and sheer curtains. Weigh your curtains and rod before you hang them rather than guessing, and for any rod longer than about 120 cm (48 in), use at least three hooks, one at each end and one in the centre, to spread the load.</p>

